BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:culture360-asef-arts-in-the-wake-of-ai-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261002T125318Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260929
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260930
SUMMARY:Deadline: culture360 / ASEF: Call for Content\, Arts in the Wake of
  AI
DESCRIPTION:culture360.ASEF.org\, the arts platform of the Asia-Europe Foun
 dation\, invites proposals from writers and content producers for its open
  call 'Arts in the Wake of AI': articles\, podcasts and video interviews t
 hat foreground how cultural professionals and organisations across Asia an
 d Europe are adapting to AI technologies\, from generative tools and machi
 ne learning to recommender systems and algorithms. WHAT THEY WANT: case-st
 udy-based contributions grounded in real-world evidence\, experiences or p
 ractices\, on topics including the impact of AI on cultural diversity\, fa
 irness and inclusivity\; the environmental costs of AI in the cultural sec
 tor\; and AI governance in the cultural sector (policy\, copyright\, intel
 lectual property and the ethical use of AI-generated cultural content). Ac
 ademic research papers and non-arts subjects are not considered. FEE: SGD 
 250 per commissioned article\, podcast or video interview. FORMATS: journa
 listic articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four credited images\
 ; podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes. ELIGIBILITY: national
 s of ASEM partner countries with previous experience in writing\, podcasti
 ng or video production\, proficiency in English\, and knowledge of or invo
 lvement in the Asian and European arts and culture scene\; an existing cha
 nnel for further dissemination is an advantage\; the work is fully virtual
 . Up to two proposals per application. Only selected writers are notified.
  Queries to culture[at]asef.org. Deadline 29 September 2026 at 6 PM Singap
 ore time\, via the online application form.\n\nOrganisation: Asia-Europe F
 oundation (ASEF)\, culture360.ASEF.org\nLocation: Fully remote\; contribut
 ors do not need to be based in Singapore. Open to nationals of ASEM partne
 r countries across Asia and Europe.\nAward: SGD 250 fee per commissioned a
 rticle\, podcast or video interview. Up to two proposals per application\,
  of which at most one is selected. No application fee.\nDuration: One-off 
 editorial commissions: articles of 800 to 1\,000 words with at least four 
 credited pictures\, podcasts and video interviews of 15 to 20 minutes\n\nM
 ore: https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://culture360.asef.org/opportunities/call-for-content-arts-ai/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully remote\; contributors do not need to be based in Singapore. 
 Open to nationals of ASEM partner countries across Asia and Europe.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261002T125318Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:roosevelt-good-life-resident-ai-finance-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261002T125318Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: Good Life Residents Program: AI and Finance
DESCRIPTION:The Good Life Residents Program is a six-month Roosevelt Instit
 ute initiative that develops new leaders and elevates bold ideas across si
 x economic policy priorities under its Good Life Agenda. Each topic area h
 osts a cohort of 3 to 4 residents plus 1 to 2 senior Topic Advisors (acade
 mics\, policy leaders\, organizers or government practitioners) who provid
 e strategic and intellectual mentorship and moderate the closing event. Re
 sidents write a roughly 1\,500-word original policy essay advancing a peop
 le-centered policy vision against a topic-specific prompt\, take part in s
 tructured dialogue with peers\, Topic Advisors and Roosevelt Think Tank di
 rectors\, help prepare and appear at a culminating public event\, contribu
 te to press opportunities such as op-eds and reporter calls where applicab
 le\, and act as ambassadors for the Good Life Agenda in their professional
  and public networks. The current round\, the AI and Finance Residents Pro
 gram\, is run in partnership with Data & Society and asks for bold ideas o
 n how to shape\, redirect\, interrupt or make more visible and contestable
  the flows of capital into generative AI. Framing: breakneck investment in
  generative AI technology and infrastructure is distorting the stock marke
 t\, profit measures\, bond issuance\, the pace and composition of growth\,
  job creation and trade policy\, with seven tech companies accounting for 
 more than a third of S&P 500 market capitalisation and complex\, sometimes
  circular financial arrangements obscuring risk. Suggested lines of inquir
 y include what government interventions could improve public oversight\, r
 isk allocation and direction-setting in private markets\; how other invest
 ments could be coordinated and scaled if AI spending is crowding them out\
 ; what role public spending or new institutions could play in catalysing t
 echnology R&D designed to support workers\, sustainability and supply chai
 ns\; what democratic input mechanisms would keep state influence over capi
 tal flows less vulnerable to corruption\; and how federal AI policy should
  address public hostility to data centre investments alongside national se
 curity and competitiveness. Compensation is USD 3\,000 plus travel and acc
 ommodation for the culminating event. The AI and Finance round closed 12 A
 ugust 2026 (deadline per Opportunity Desk\; Roosevelt's own programme page
  did not state it). Earlier rounds (Social Security financing\, Federal Re
 serve powers\, housing) each ran their own short application window\, so a
  future round may open with a new deadline.\n\nOrganisation: Roosevelt Ins
 titute\, in partnership with Data & Society\nLocation: Remote. Travel may 
 be requested for select convenings and for the culminating public event. U
 S economic policy focus.\nAward: USD 3\,000 stipend\, plus travel and acco
 mmodation for the culminating event if needed. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: 6 months\, part-time at roughly 5 to 8 hours per month\n\nMore: https:
 //rooseveltinstitute.org/careers/good-life-resident-program/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://rooseveltinstitute.org/careers/good-life-resident-program/?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. Travel may be requested for select convenings and for the 
 culminating public event. US economic policy focus.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kennan-institute-digital-fellowship-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20261002T125318Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kennan Institute Digital Fellowship 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:The inaugural Kennan Institute Digital Fellowship promotes orig
 inal scholarship and quality non-fiction writing about Russia and the form
 er Soviet Union\, with an emphasis on work that has policy relevance. Ten 
 fellowships are awarded per year\, and AT LEAST FOUR POSITIONS ARE RESERVE
 D FOR RESEARCHERS PURSUING TOPICS IN THE NATURAL SCIENCES\, MEDICINE\, TEC
 HNOLOGY OR RELATED FIELDS\, which is the route by which a technology-focus
 ed project fits this call. The programme aims to give participants time\, 
 professional resources and support while bringing their work to a wider pu
 blic and to the Washington-based policy community\, and to build a cohort 
 network that continues into the alumni body. Each fellow receives a monthl
 y stipend of USD 2\,000\, mentorship and consultation from the Kennan comm
 unity of experts where applicable\, six virtual webinars tailored to the c
 ohort's needs\, and membership of the alumni network. ELIGIBILITY: intende
 d for individuals with several years of experience working on\, studying o
 r otherwise examining Russia and/or broader Eurasia\; applicants must have
  a good working knowledge of English\, as the fellowship is conducted only
  in English and any publications produced for the Kennan Institute appear 
 first in English with possible later translation into regional languages. 
 The programme runs entirely virtually from 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027
 \; the Institute does not cover travel-related expenses including visas. S
 upported by the Lounsbery Foundation and the U.S. Russia Foundation. Deadl
 ine 14 August 2026\, 17:00 EST. NOTE: 'digital' here describes the virtual
  format\, not the subject matter\, so this fits the desk only if your proj
 ect is itself about technology\, data or science.\n\nOrganisation: Kennan 
 Institute\, Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars (supported by
  the Lounsbery Foundation and the U.S. Russia Foundation)\nLocation: Fully
  virtual. No travel is funded and no travel is required.\nAward: USD 2\,00
 0 monthly stipend for the six-month programme\, plus mentorship from the K
 ennan community of experts\, six virtual webinars tailored to the cohort\,
  and inclusion in the Kennan alumni network of over 4\,500 people. Travel 
 and visa costs are explicitly not covered. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 Six months\, 1 October 2026 to 31 March 2027\, followed by several months 
 of possible integration into the Kennan Institute's event schedule\n\nMore
 : https://www.kennaninstitute.org/digital-fellowship?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.kennaninstitute.org/digital-fellowship?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fully virtual. No travel is funded and no travel is required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bulletin-editorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261002T125318Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists Editorial Fellowship 20
 26
DESCRIPTION:The Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists runs an Editorial Fellows
  Program for emerging leaders who can give its audience regular and expans
 ive coverage of the Bulletin's core issues\, the existential threats facin
 g humanity. TRACKS: two specialty tracks are open in this round\, biosecur
 ity and disruptive technology/AI. Fellows write for the Bulletin's editori
 al pages\, producing up to four published pieces over the year at USD 750 
 each\, with limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses covere
 d on approval. WHO SHOULD APPLY: this is not a student programme. Applican
 ts need significant professional experience\, an established record of pub
 lishing quality work\, and recognition as an emerging leader in their fiel
 d\; undergraduate and graduate students may apply only if they bring subst
 antial writing experience and published work. No geographic or citizenship
  restriction is stated. HOW TO APPLY: email a resume\, a cover letter (the
  call stresses that the cover letter matters)\, and up to three writing sa
 mples\, with 'editorial fellow' as the subject line\, to the address given
  on the call page. Fellows are announced in September 2026. Deadline 28 Au
 gust 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists\nLocation: R
 emote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.\nAward: USD 750
  per published piece\, for up to four pieces annually\, so up to USD 3\,00
 0. Limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses are also covere
 d. No application fee.\nDuration: Annual fellowship\, built around up to f
 our published pieces\n\nMore: https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-
 a-2026-bulletin-editorial-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-a-2026-bulletin-editorial-f
 ellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:briarpatch-ai-resistance-winter-2027-pitches@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20261002T125318Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260918
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260919
SUMMARY:Deadline: Briarpatch Magazine: AI Resistance Issue\, Winter 2027 Ca
 ll for Pitches
DESCRIPTION:Briarpatch\, the Canadian magazine of grassroots politics\, ded
 icates its Winter 2027 issue to AI Resistance and is paying for articles\,
  investigative reporting\, historical analysis\, photo essays\, timelines\
 , profiles\, reading lists and art. SUGGESTED ANGLES include Indigenous-le
 d and local resistance to data centres\, exploitation and outsourcing of l
 abour in LLM training\, AI's impact on work and union resistance\, data mi
 ning and data sovereignty\, environmental and climate impacts\, artists an
 d AI (crisis of creativity and ownership)\, the language and inevitability
  narratives of AI\, policing and the surveillance state\, AI and disinform
 ation\, and investigations into Big Tech lobbying and media influence in C
 anada. PITCH: outline what ground your contribution covers\, estimated wor
 d count\, and your relevant experience\; first-time Briarpatch writers sho
 uld add a brief writing sample. Send to pitch [at] briarpatchmagazine.com.
  Accepted first drafts are due 14 October 2026 with collaborative editing 
 until late November\; publication is not guaranteed. No pitch fee. Deadlin
 e 18 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Briarpatch Magazine\nLocation: Regin
 a\, Canada (remote pitching\; Canadian focus)\nAward: CAD 150 for profiles
 \, short essays\, book reviews and parting shots (under ~1\,500 words)\; C
 AD 250 for feature stories and photo essays\; CAD 350 for research-based a
 rticles and investigative reporting (2\,000-2\,500 words). No pitch fee.\n
 Duration: First drafts due 14 October 2026\, editing through late November
 \n\nMore: https://briarpatchmagazine.com/winter-2027-pitches?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://briarpatchmagazine.com/winter-2027-pitches?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Regina\, Canada (remote pitching\; Canadian focus)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:open-notebook-early-career-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261002T125318Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Open Notebook Early-Career Fellowship 2027 (remote)
DESCRIPTION:The Open Notebook's paid fellowship for early-career science jo
 urnalists is open for its 2027 round. Fellows spend twelve months working 
 part-time and remotely with a personal mentor\, reporting and writing arti
 cles about the craft of science journalism that are published at The Open 
 Notebook\, one of the field's central craft resources. Eligibility: early-
 career science journalists with fewer than three years of regular professi
 onal science-writing experience\; applicants from anywhere in the world ar
 e welcome\, and application materials are in English. TO APPLY: submit the
  application form (linked from the program page) by 31 October 2026\, 11:5
 9 PM US Central time\; reference letters are due 7 November 2026. The fell
 owship year starts 9 February 2027.\n\nOrganisation: The Open Notebook\nLo
 cation: Remote\, worldwide. Application materials in English\; internation
 al applicants welcome.\nAward: USD 6\,600 stipend over the twelve-month pa
 rt-time fellowship\, plus one-on-one mentoring from an experienced science
  journalist and published clips at The Open Notebook. No application fee.\
 nDuration: 12 months part-time\, starting 9 February 2027.\n\nMore: https:
 //www.theopennotebook.com/early-career-fellowship-program/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.theopennotebook.com/early-career-fellowship-program/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, worldwide. Application materials in English\; internation
 al applicants welcome.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
